From d7433ac2d9a9beb526a484b2861a96987f3ed046 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 15:51:55 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] tuntap: utun implementation for Darwin

The UE data path opens a TUN interface for every PDU session through
tuntap_if.c, which was written on the Linux tun driver and, on Darwin,
reported failure for every call since the first port step.

Darwin has utun instead: a kernel control socket (PF_SYSTEM,
SYSPROTO_CONTROL, com.apple.net.utun_control). The kernel assigns the
unit and the name, so the name OAI generates (oaitun_ue1p<n>) cannot be
applied; tuntap_if.c keeps a table from the requested name to the utun
the kernel handed out, and the configuration and teardown calls that
follow look the real name up. The address goes on with SIOCAIFADDR as
address, destination (the broadaddr slot, the kernel reads it as the
destination of a point to point interface) and mask; that gives the host
route, any further route is set up outside. IPv6, TAP and the MBMS
tunnel are reported as not implemented. Opening a utun requires root.

Every packet on a utun carries a 4-byte address family in host order in
front of the IP header. tuntap_read() and tuntap_write() strip and add
it; on Linux they are read(2) and write(2). The SDAP reader and writer
and the LTE PDCP writer use them instead of the raw calls. The flag
values IFF_TUN and IFF_TAP come from linux/if_tun.h on Linux and are
defined in tuntap_if.h elsewhere; the interface-flag helpers are shared
by both implementations.
---
